        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Film thickness"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                98,
                78,
                267,
                95
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "The film thickness was estimated from XPS data using deconvolution of the metallic and oxide part of the Cr 2p3/2 peak (10,11). The results obtained for the two alloys polarised in the electrolytes (A) and (B) are shown in Fig.6. The apparent film thickness is not significantly affected by the presence or not of chloride ion in the electrolyte or of molybdenum in the alloy. The film thickness of the samples that were cathodically polarised (CA) is that of the oxide film formed spontaneously during sample handling and transfer. The films formed during anodic passivation are only slightly thicker than the films observed on the cathodically polarised samples."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                96,
                111,
                886,
                287
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "chart",
            "content": {
                "image_source": {
                    "path": "images/23a455867a733a4a4cfdde0c32fbec85ba9008d9483a4cda9ce04d97bdd4cbae.jpg"
                },
                "content": "",
                "chart_caption": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Fig.6 Apparent Film Thickness Calculated from Cr 2p3/2 XPS Peaks"
                    }
                ],
                "chart_footnote": []
            },
            "bbox": [
                254,
                321,
                762,
                609
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "title",
            "content": {
                "title_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Chloride ion analysis"
                    }
                ],
                "level": 2
            },
            "bbox": [
                100,
                718,
                335,
                736
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "AES profiles were determined using a rastered 1 KeV Kr+ ion beam. Measured profiles were numerically corrected for the effect of the finite escape depth of the Auger electrons (10,11). Fig. 7 shows the chloride depth profile ( LMM peak at 184 eV) measured in solution B on the Fe24Cr alloy. The cathodically polarised sample shows a measurable chloride signal only at the surface. This signal is attributed to adsorbed anions that remained after the rinsing procedure. The samples polarised in the passive potential region exhibit a chloride maximum inside the film, at an apparent depth of 0.3-0.4 nm. This suggests that the passive films formed under these conditions contained chloride. A rough estimation using sensitivity factors indicates a maximum concentration of less than 1 at%. On the Fe24Cr11Mo alloy an AES chloride profile could not be determined because of overlap of the LMM Ci peak at 181 eV with the MNV Mo peak at 186 eV."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                96,
                756,
                886,
                914
            ]
        },
        {
