                "content": "",
                "chart_caption": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Fig. 7 AES Depth Profile of CI Peak on Fe24Cr Alloy Polarised at CA, PL and PH"
                    }
                ],
                "chart_footnote": []
            },
            "bbox": [
                147,
                203,
                773,
                474
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Further information was obtained from SIMS profiles. Fig.8 shows negative SIMS profiles of the peak of the mass/charge ratio of 35 corresponding to chloride ion. The intensity of the profiles is normalised to the same height in order to better show the differences in depth distribution. The SIMS profiles of the Fe24Cr ailoy qualitatively confirm the results of the AES depth profile analysis. On the cathodically polarised sample chloride is observed only at the oxide surface but on the passivated samples a chloride maximum occurs within the film."
                    }
                ]
            },
            "bbox": [
                98,
                597,
                891,
                756
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": {
                "paragraph_content": [
                    {
                        "type": "text",
                        "content": "Analogous data for the Fe24Cr11Mo alloy reveal no significant difference in the position of the Cl maximum between the cathodically polarised sample CA and the anodically polarised sample PL. On the other hand, the CI maximum for the anodically polarised sample PH is shifted slightly to the right and is situated at a depth of approx 0.2-0.3 nm. This is less than the value of 0.3-0.4 nm found by SIMS and by AES for the Fe24Cr alloy. It appears from these data that less chloride ion is present in the passive film formed on the molybdenum containing alloy than on the alloy without molybdenum."
                    }
                ]
            },
